Negative control for the port refusing an actor the module's own matrix marks
`never` (`R7-14`).

Drops the matrix gate and lets every url-named actor boot a cell, which is the
pre-ruling shape: `useClientEmails().as('guest')` builds fine, the module then
resolves its request target from the ACTIVE SESSION rather than from the url,
and the guest scope answers with the signed-in client's identity — the same
cache entry, the same four rows. Nothing is disabled and nothing throws: the
port still publishes a snapshot, so only the refusal law is on trial.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the port's guest-scope case — a port booted at `/as/guest` no longer
publishes `isServed: false`, gains the cell's actions and its `data` context,
and the page draws the client's rows instead of the not-available notice. The
`/as/client` case still passes, so the control cannot go red by refusing
everything.

